														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1410565">
									

<p>short version: felines are small cats, felids are ALL cats. longer version: big cats are pantherines, and necessarily not felines (pantherinae and felinae are the two subfamilies of family felidae). small cats are everything that isn't a lion, tiger, jaguar, or leopard of some sort - pumas, lynxes, cheetahs, cervals, anything with &quot;cat&quot; at the end are felines.</p>
<p>in the main part of the article, i would suggest replacing all but the third instance of &quot;feline&quot; with &quot;felid,&quot; and substituting &quot;pantherines&quot; for &quot;Larger felines&quot;. the three instances in the test log are correct, though you might want to test a young pantherine for thoroughness. also, i *personally* would make the lynx behave like a house cat, rather than a big cat, but that's just me trying to make the behavioral distinction phylogenetically tidy.</p>

							</div>

								
